---
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import RecipeCard from '../../../components/RecipeCard.astro'

const clip = `name: order-total

clip:
  frame: { css: 'iframe[title="Checkout"]' }
  css: '.order-total'
  pad: 16`

const mark = `name: checkout

clip: { css: '.checkout-panel' }

marks:
  card:
    frame: { css: 'iframe[title="Payment"]' }
    label: Card number

callouts:
  - { mark: card, text: Your card is never sent to us, place: right }`

const crossOrigin = `site:
  allow:
    - checkout.example.com`

const drive = `setup:
  - fill:
      frame: { css: 'iframe[title="Payment"]' }
      label: Card number
    value: '4242 4242 4242 4242'
  - wait:
      frame: { css: 'iframe[title="Payment"]' }
      text: Card accepted`

const nested = `marks:
  total:
    frame: { css: 'iframe[title="Checkout"]' }
    css: '.total'`

const settle = `setup:
  - click: { role: button, name: Add a block }
  - wait:
      frame: { css: 'iframe[name="editor-canvas"]' }
      text: Media Library
  - wait: 1500
    comment: the application rebuilt its frame, and the new one has not painted yet`
---

<Docs
  title="How to capture an element inside an iframe"
  description="Point a clip, a mark or a step at something inside an iframe with the frame key, including cross-origin frames."
>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold">Capture an element inside an iframe</h1>

  <p class="text-lg">
    An iframe is a separate document, and a query does not cross into one on its own. This guide
    names the iframe so everything after it resolves inside — a payment field, an embedded checkout,
    a block editor's canvas.
  </p>

  <p>Requires shotlist 0.4.0 or later.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Clip a region inside a frame</h2>

  <p>
    <code>frame</code> takes a query of its own, which finds the <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> element.
    Everything written beside it then resolves in that iframe's document:
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={clip} file="screenshots/recipes/order-total.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    Name the iframe by something it carries — a <code>title</code>, a <code>name</code>, an
    <code>id</code>. Those are attributes on the element in the page you opened, so an ordinary
    query finds them.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Mark something inside a frame</h2>

  <p>
    A mark takes <code>frame</code> the same way, and it does not have to be the region you clipped. Here
    the capture is a panel in the page and the callout points at a field inside an embedded payment form:
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={mark} file="screenshots/recipes/checkout.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    The rect comes back in page coordinates, so the callout lands where a reader sees the field
    rather than where the frame thinks it is.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Click and type inside a frame</h2>

  <p>Steps take queries, so nothing new is needed:</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={drive} file="screenshots/recipes/checkout.yaml" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Cross-origin frames</h2>

  <p>Add the frame's destination to <code>site.allow</code>:</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={crossOrigin} file="shotlist.config.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    Resolution goes through the browser rather than the page's own JavaScript, so a query can reach
    into the approved frame even though the page containing it cannot. The destination approval is
    separate because the frame makes its own network requests rather than inheriting permission from
    the page around it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">A frame inside a frame</h2>

  <p>
    Needs nothing extra. Name the inner one and the coordinates still come back against the page:
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={nested} file="screenshots/recipes/order-total.yaml" />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">When the frame is empty in the picture</h2>

  <p>
    An application that renders its own frame — a block editor, a live preview — often replaces it
    when its content changes. A query resolves against the frame that is there when it runs, and the
    replacement has not painted yet, so the capture comes out blank while the callout sits in
    exactly the right place.
  </p>

  <p>Wait for the content, then let it settle:</p>

  <div class="mt-6">
    <RecipeCard code={settle} file="screenshots/recipes/editor.yaml" />
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>site.settle</code> does the same for every recipe in the project, which is the better home for
    it once more than one shot needs it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">What it does not handle</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      A <code>transform</code> or <code>zoom</code> on the iframe element is not compensated for, so a
      scaled preview places its callouts off the mark.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>within</code> naming a mark outside the frame is refused rather than quietly scoped to nothing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">Related</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/reference/queries/#frames">Queries reference: <code>frame</code></a></li>
    <li><a href="/docs/how-to/drive-a-page/">Capture a page after interaction</a></li>
    <li><a href="/docs/explanation/durable-queries/">Queries that survive a redesign</a></li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
